00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:09.190
Now, back to the show.

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:11.690
Sci-fi has always
inspired real-world tech.

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:13.280
What's arguably the first sci-fi movie,

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:15.660
Fritz Lang's 1929 film
"Woman in the Moon,"

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:18.880
helped humanity add space
flight to its to-do list.

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:20.860
Rocketry pioneer Hermann Oberth

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:23.020
consulted with Lang to
envision the space shuttle,

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:25.430
bringing in ideas like multi-stage rockets

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:26.880
and the take-off countdown that,

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:28.860
for both practical and aesthetic reasons,

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:31.280
have become foundational to space travel.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:34.520
The movie was also beloved
by America's Pet Nazi,

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:35.760
Wernher von Braun,

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:38.270
who went on to design
the Apollo spacecraft.

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:39.830
In the wider view, countless figures

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:41.370
across science, tech, and industry,

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:42.760
including Edwin Hubble,

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:46.580
cite early sci-fi novelist Jules
Verne as a key inspiration.

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:48.900
One of the Apollo 8
astronauts, Frank Borman,

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:50.650
even went so far as to say

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:52.310
that, "In a very real sense,

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:55.380
Jules Verne is one of the
pioneers of the space age."

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:57.430
Today's technologists are no different;

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:00.570
they invariably cite both
classic and contemporary sci-fi

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:02.000
as formative inspirations.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:03.050
Much of Silicon Valley

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:05.250
treats the Neal Stephenson
novel, "Snow Crash"

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:07.580
as some kind of holy text.

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:09.640
That includes Mark Zuckerburg,

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:12.470
who apparently made it required
reading for new recruits.

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:15.540
Elon Musk says that Isaac
Asimov's "Foundation"

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:17.540
was key to his founding SpaceX,

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:18.770
and is open about his love

00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:21.527
of Douglas Adams' "Hitchhiker's
Guide to the Galaxy" series.

00:03:21.527 --> 00:03:24.180
And Peter Thiel has described
his vision of the future

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:28.060
as uniquely inspired by sci-fi
writer Robert A. Heinlein.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:31.330
It's no surprise that an
industry dominated by nerds

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:32.920
has some geeky shared interests.

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:34.690
But while sci-fi is a wide genre,

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:38.250
some works are invoked
a lot more than others.

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:40.380
In addition to the aforementioned
"Foundation" series

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:41.213
and "Snow Crash,"

00:03:41.213 --> 00:03:43.600
"Star Trek" rounds out the big three.

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:45.397
Perhaps more so than any other work,

00:03:45.397 --> 00:03:47.880
"Snow Crash" has wielded
incredible influence

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:48.920
over Silicon Valley.

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:50.640
Everyone who's anyone in tech

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:52.520
has named it as a big inspiration,

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:55.320
from Google co-founders
Sergey Brin and Larry Page,

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:57.800
to Peter Thiel, and,
perhaps most fatefully,

00:03:57.800 --> 00:03:58.820
Mark Zuckerburg.

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:01.480
It's easy to see why
they're all so keen on it.

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:04.020
A key part of the novel's
world is the Metaverse,

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:06.050
a sort of digital wild west

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:08.620
where coders and techies reign supreme.

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:11.630
With just a VR headset and a
steady internet connection,

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:14.070
people can live almost
entirely in the Metaverse,

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:17.320
coming out only for short
periods to do dumb (beep),

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:20.340
like eat and learn their children's names.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:22.060
And, if people like
The Zucc get their way,

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:25.340
the Metaverse will soon become
a thing we can't ignore.

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:28.080
He even changed his company's name to Meta

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.330
to show just how committed he is.

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:32.740
And game platforms like
Fortnite and Roblox

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:34.270
are both inspired by,

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:37.560
and trying to manifest their
own versions of, the Metaverse.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:38.990
Then, there's "Foundation,"

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:41.260
which Elon Musk, in
particular, has lauded,

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:44.040
calling it fundamental to
the creation of SpaceX.

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:45.310
The TL;DR of "Foundation"

00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:47.980
is one big-brain man is so good at math

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:50.210
that he can accurately predict the future.

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:52.980
He discovers that the
Galactic Empire is doomed,

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:55.930
so he takes a bunch of the best
scientists to another planet

00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:59.410
to build a new Empire: a
scientific meritocracy.

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:01.700
The series is rooted in that old chestnut,

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:03.430
American exceptionalism,

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:04.570
which, as your seventh-grade

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:06.020
social studies teacher told you,

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:08.850
basically just means
America is uniquely great.

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:11.370
This notion especially took
off after World War II,

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:13.700
where America established its dominance.

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:14.597
In its early chapters,

00:05:14.597 --> 00:05:17.010
"Foundation" seems to endorse this view.

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:20.240
But Asimov wrote the "Foundation"
series over a long period,

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:22.930
with a nearly three-decade
gap in the middle,

00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:25.120
and the core ideas he communicated changed

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:26.500
to reflect the times.

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:27.507
By the end of the series,

00:05:27.507 --> 00:05:30.500
"Foundation" had challenged
the post-war ideas at its core,

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:33.620
exploring the follies of
imperialism and exceptionalism

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:36.100
within the context of the
Second Galactic Empire.

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:38.730
Basically, the protagonist's initial goal

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:41.590
of single-handedly starting this new world

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:43.180
is sharply critiqued.

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:44.960
In fact, Asimov made it well-known

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:47.190
that he based the
overall arc of the series

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:49.110
on "The History of the Decline and Fall

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:50.220
of the Roman Empire."

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:52.430
But the big boys of Silicon Valley

00:05:52.430 --> 00:05:54.560
also have some more
mainstream sci-fi interests.

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:56.040
Take "Star Trek."

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:59.060
We probably don't need to
explain this classic to you,

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:01.210
with its utopian Federation of Planets

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:04.190
and exploration of complex
social and philosophical issues.

00:06:04.190 --> 00:06:06.300
It's a series defined by its idealism

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:09.330
and total stanning of
justice and equality.

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:10.900
People who worked with Steve Jobs

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:12.210
named his ability to push you

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:13.960
to do seemingly impossible things

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:16.030
his reality distortion field,

00:06:16.030 --> 00:06:18.030
a term straight out of the "Trek."

00:06:18.030 --> 00:06:20.120
And real-life Scrooge McDuck, Jeff Bezos,

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:21.813
has been outspoken about the influence

00:06:21.813 --> 00:06:23.780
that the show has had on him.

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:27.050
He almost named Amazon makeitso.com,

00:06:27.050 --> 00:06:29.290
after Picard's famous catchphrase.

00:06:29.290 --> 00:06:30.190
- Make it so.

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:32.050
- And the complex
voice-controlled computers

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:35.540
of the "Enterprise" were a
direct inspiration for Alexa.

00:06:35.540 --> 00:06:37.140
Perhaps most tellingly,

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:39.370
Bezos ended his high
school valedictorian speech

00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:40.570
with a "Star Trek" quote.

00:06:40.570 --> 00:06:43.960
He said, "Space, the final frontier.

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:44.953
Meet me there."

00:06:44.953 --> 00:06:47.750
Bezos also tacitly forced William Shatner

00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:49.790
to look at his childhood "Trek" fan art.

00:06:49.790 --> 00:06:53.090
Billionaires: they're just
as embarrassing as us!

00:06:53.090 --> 00:06:55.920
So we've established
the enormous influence

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:57.930
sci-fi has had on Silicon Valley.

00:06:57.930 --> 00:06:59.810
And hey, in some ways,

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:02.690
knowing that these people
enjoy this genre is kinda cool.

00:07:02.690 --> 00:07:04.550
And when some of the most
powerful people in the world

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:05.390
are sci-fi nerds,

00:07:05.390 --> 00:07:07.710
it's going to have an
impact on the culture.

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:10.460
But even if they share the
interests of the hoi polloi,

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:13.600
their lives are so radically
different from ours

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:16.440
that they may be taking away
something totally different

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:18.330
from our shared sci-fi faves.

00:07:18.330 --> 00:07:20.000
Let's go back to "Snow Crash,"

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:22.640
which is set in a real-world dystopia.

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:24.680
Everyone lives in Burbclaves,

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:25.737
which are described as,

00:07:25.737 --> 00:07:28.440
"A city-state with its own constitution:

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:30.820
a border, laws, cops, everything."

00:07:30.820 --> 00:07:34.190
Everything within these
Burbclaves has been corporatized,

00:07:34.190 --> 00:07:37.780
including the law, meaning
that big corporations can, say,

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:40.600
legally kill you for minor
criminal infractions.

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:42.490
So if you work for say, Amazon,

00:07:42.490 --> 00:07:44.610
you would likely live
in the Amazon Burbclave.

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:46.350
In the world of "Snow Crash,"

00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:48.720
corporations basically have full control

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:50.480
over government and society;

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:52.800
not that this could ever
happen in our world.

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:54.720
And this has forced those seeking freedom

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:55.920
into the Metaverse,

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:58.180
a place free from corporate control.

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:01.050
It's essentially an anarchist technocracy,

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:03.030
and the only boundaries are set

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:05.210
by the technology you have access to.

00:08:05.210 --> 00:08:07.600
This is where companies
like Meta miss the point.

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:10.980
Because even if other companies
contribute to the metaverse,

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:13.970
Meta is always going to have more control.

00:08:13.970 --> 00:08:15.310
If they host the servers,

00:08:15.310 --> 00:08:18.170
if their infrastructure
is what it's built upon,

00:08:18.170 --> 00:08:20.590
then the Metaverse will never be free

00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:21.900
from corporate oversight.

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:24.830
Meta isn't going to allow
anyone with enough coding chops

00:08:24.830 --> 00:08:26.600
to mess around with the source code

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:29.370
because the company has
invested time, money,

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:31.520
and brainpower into its design.

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:33.670
The Metaverse of Zuckerberg's dream

00:08:33.670 --> 00:08:34.950
is one that's been divvied up

00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:38.210
into multiple, corporate-controlled
walled gardens,

00:08:38.210 --> 00:08:40.970
each with their own terms
of service and currencies.

00:08:40.970 --> 00:08:42.380
This ironically resembles

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:45.200
the hellish corporate
reality of "Snow Crash"

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:47.740
more than it's anarchist
digital Metaverse.

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:50.980
Here, a tech billionaire
with the unique ability

00:08:50.980 --> 00:08:53.170
to turn a sci-fi vision into reality

00:08:53.170 --> 00:08:55.170
seems to have missed the critique

00:08:55.170 --> 00:08:57.170
at the heart of that fictional vision.

00:08:57.170 --> 00:08:58.760
And this isn't the only example

00:08:58.760 --> 00:09:00.960
of a tech bro missing some vital points

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:02.320
from their favorite content.

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:03.550
Take "Foundation."

00:09:03.550 --> 00:09:06.010
When Musk talks about
escaping climate change

00:09:06.010 --> 00:09:07.930
by moving humanity to Mars,

00:09:07.930 --> 00:09:12.640
a feat that only he is brave
or willing enough to undertake,

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:14.320
it's easy to see the connection.

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:17.510
Musk gets to step into the
role of the big-brain man

00:09:17.510 --> 00:09:19.180
who anticipates our doom

00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:21.590
and works as a private
entity to solve the problem.

00:09:21.590 --> 00:09:23.360
But in being the man who built,

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:27.320
and therefore owns and operates
his proposed Martian colony,

00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:31.170
Musk also takes the role of
the head of the meritocracy.

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:34.480
He contributed the
most, so he's in charge.

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:37.200
However, in painfully ironic news,

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:38.510
that's the very concept

00:09:38.510 --> 00:09:41.450
that the "Foundation"
series explicitly disavows.

00:09:41.450 --> 00:09:43.370
But in Musk's fantasy,

00:09:43.370 --> 00:09:45.260
he is the exceptional man

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:49.870
with both an idea and the
scientific know-how to realize it.

00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:52.110
Every launch that SpaceX undertakes

00:09:52.110 --> 00:09:53.550
has the underlying goal

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:55.670
of building towards Mars' colonization.

00:09:55.670 --> 00:09:58.970
But a one-man-led colonization
mission in Foundation

00:09:58.970 --> 00:10:02.850
isn't the solution, it's a
recipe for social collapse.

00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:04.560
So, not exactly something

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:06.000
the average tech billionaire

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:08.450
should be looking to imitate, right?

00:10:08.450 --> 00:10:10.010
But we do have one tech bro

00:10:10.010 --> 00:10:13.610
whose role model is
objectively a pretty good guy,

00:10:13.610 --> 00:10:15.890
and a true bald king.

00:10:15.890 --> 00:10:18.120
Jeff Bezos is such a big Trekker

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:21.480
that he says he idolizes
"Enterprise" captain

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:22.380
Jean-Luc Picard.

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:24.880
Which is interesting,
because Picard is defined

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:27.350
by his strong sense of ethics and morals,

00:10:27.350 --> 00:10:30.690
arguably even more than
other captains in the series.

00:10:30.690 --> 00:10:33.422
Whereas Kirk is brash and headstrong,

00:10:33.422 --> 00:10:35.520
Picard is measured and deliberate.

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:38.160
Kirk will make mistakes and
do the right thing eventually,

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:40.890
while Picard will
agonize over the decision

00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:43.540
to avoid making the
mistake in the first place.

00:10:43.540 --> 00:10:44.830
Now, we're not claiming

00:10:44.830 --> 00:10:47.540
that Bezos doesn't
consider all his options

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:48.770
before making a decision.

00:10:48.770 --> 00:10:51.170
You don't get to be a
big-time capitalist baron

00:10:51.170 --> 00:10:52.510
without thinking things through.

00:10:52.510 --> 00:10:54.170
But there is a disconnect

00:10:54.170 --> 00:10:56.540
between the example that Picard sets,

00:10:56.540 --> 00:10:58.980
and Bezos' public-facing actions.

00:10:58.980 --> 00:11:02.010
Here's a couple of Picard
quotes to show what we mean.

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:03.950
In "First Contact," he says...

00:11:03.950 --> 00:11:05.480
- The acquisition of wealth

00:11:05.480 --> 00:11:08.023
is no longer the driving
force of our lives.

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:11.400
We work to better ourselves

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:14.453
and the rest of humanity.

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:17.670
- In "Next Generation," he says...

00:11:17.670 --> 00:11:20.840
- The first time any man's freedom

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:23.083
is trodden on,

00:11:24.140 --> 00:11:25.133
we're all damaged.

00:11:26.180 --> 00:11:28.350
- And also in "Next Generation,"

00:11:28.350 --> 00:11:30.550
- I have to weigh the good of the many

00:11:31.690 --> 00:11:33.920
against the needs of the individual,

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:38.110
and try to balance them as
realistically as possible.

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:39.690
- These aren't outliers;

00:11:39.690 --> 00:11:42.790
they all demonstrate Picard's
fundamental character.

00:11:42.790 --> 00:11:46.520
He's all about altruism and
bettering mankind at large.

00:11:46.520 --> 00:11:50.560
In contrast, Jeff Bezos has a
literally unspendable amount

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:53.060
of personal wealth,
which he keeps adding to.

00:11:53.060 --> 00:11:53.893
- It was fine being

00:11:53.893 --> 00:11:55.630
the second-wealthiest person in the world.

00:11:55.630 --> 00:11:57.422
That actually worked fine.

00:11:57.422 --> 00:11:59.630
- And Amazon has roundly been exposed

00:11:59.630 --> 00:12:00.980
for exploiting workers.

00:12:00.980 --> 00:12:03.250
Regardless of your stance on those issues

00:12:03.250 --> 00:12:05.050
and whether or not you agree with Picard,

00:12:05.050 --> 00:12:07.790
Bezos' actions are clearly contradictory

00:12:07.790 --> 00:12:09.710
to the fictional man he idolizes.

00:12:09.710 --> 00:12:11.870
If Picard were real and he met Bezos,

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:14.780
it's clear he'd bar his
application to the Federation.

00:12:14.780 --> 00:12:18.380
So who cares if figures like
Musk, Zuckerburg, and Bezos

00:12:18.380 --> 00:12:21.670
are big old sci-fi nerds with
poor critical reading skills?

00:12:21.670 --> 00:12:23.990
Honestly, everybody should,

00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:27.220
due to their outsize influence
on government and society.

00:12:27.220 --> 00:12:29.300
This implication is especially stark

00:12:29.300 --> 00:12:31.780
when you compare the wealth
of these heads of Big Tech

00:12:31.780 --> 00:12:35.130
to, say, national space
agencies like NASA,

00:12:35.130 --> 00:12:36.990
which are vastly underfunded.

00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:39.360
So our forays into the galaxy

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:41.770
are yoked into a billionaire space race

00:12:41.770 --> 00:12:44.600
that's enmeshed with the
egos of the bros involved.

00:12:44.600 --> 00:12:47.510
What's more, Big Tech has a
ridiculous amount of money

00:12:47.510 --> 00:12:48.630
to spend on lobbying,

00:12:48.630 --> 00:12:51.020
tailoring legislation to
best suit their needs,

00:12:51.020 --> 00:12:54.330
arguably at the expense of everybody else.

00:12:54.330 --> 00:12:55.410
Zuckerburg and Meta

00:12:55.410 --> 00:12:57.680
are already trying to
colonize the internet

00:12:57.680 --> 00:12:58.920
in developing nations,

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:01.430
transforming it into some VR-driven,

00:13:01.430 --> 00:13:03.360
hyper-monetized corpoland

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:04.950
that would fundamentally change

00:13:04.950 --> 00:13:06.640
how we interact with one another.

00:13:06.640 --> 00:13:09.220
Digital platforms have become a key aspect

00:13:09.220 --> 00:13:10.410
of our social interactions,

00:13:10.410 --> 00:13:11.650
and the people in charge of them

00:13:11.650 --> 00:13:14.730
have relative freedom to
do whatever they want.

00:13:14.730 --> 00:13:16.490
Zuckerberg and the Metaverse,

00:13:16.490 --> 00:13:18.430
Musk and his Mars Colonies,

00:13:18.430 --> 00:13:21.060
Bezos and wearing cowboy hats:

00:13:21.060 --> 00:13:24.030
they're all billionaire passion
projects that either are,

00:13:24.030 --> 00:13:25.400
or have the potential to,

00:13:25.400 --> 00:13:28.120
fundamentally change reality for all of us

00:13:28.120 --> 00:13:30.810
in ways both tangible and conceptual.

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:32.140
Driven by their love for,

00:13:32.140 --> 00:13:35.410
but perhaps not coherent
understanding of, sci-fi,

00:13:35.410 --> 00:13:38.180
these platforms are shaping
the world we live in,

00:13:38.180 --> 00:13:40.490
and what we imagine that
future could look like.

00:13:40.490 --> 00:13:42.030
With so much at stake,

00:13:42.030 --> 00:13:43.990
perhaps it's no wonder
that William Gibson,

00:13:43.990 --> 00:13:46.900
one of the most prophetic
sci-fi authors of all time,

00:13:46.900 --> 00:13:49.250
once said that he has explicitly decided

00:13:49.250 --> 00:13:51.380
not to write certain ideas into his books

00:13:51.380 --> 00:13:54.130
because he worried that
they'd be misunderstood,

00:13:54.130 --> 00:13:55.610
and possibly imitated.

00:13:55.610 --> 00:13:58.010
And who are we to not listen to the wisdom

00:13:58.010 --> 00:14:00.100
of the original cyberpunk?

00:14:00.100 --> 00:14:01.470
But what do you guys think?

00:14:01.470 --> 00:14:03.320
Apart from using anti-trust laws

00:14:03.320 --> 00:14:04.890
to break up tech monopolies,

00:14:04.890 --> 00:14:06.210
making them pay their taxes,

00:14:06.210 --> 00:14:08.710
and revoking all of their library cards,

00:14:08.710 --> 00:14:10.580
is there anything that can be done?

00:14:10.580 --> 00:14:11.810
Should we do anything at all

00:14:11.810 --> 00:14:15.100
or are they all free to take
what they want from sci-fi

00:14:15.100 --> 00:14:17.160
and throw away what doesn't suit?

00:14:17.160 --> 00:14:19.390
Let us know what you
think in the comments.

00:14:19.390 --> 00:14:20.760
Big thanks to all our patrons

00:14:20.760 --> 00:14:23.130
and be sure to check out our podcasts.

00:14:23.130 --> 00:14:24.380
Hit that subscribe button

00:14:24.380 --> 00:14:26.740
like you're hitting the
emergency power-off switch

00:14:26.740 --> 00:14:27.690
at the Metaverse,

00:14:27.690 --> 00:14:30.010
and don't forget to ring that bell.

00:14:30.010 --> 00:14:32.210
As always, thanks for watching,

00:14:32.210 --> 00:14:33.845
and we'll see you in space!

00:14:33.845 --> 00:14:37.345
(upbeat electronic music)

